# Line endings are LF, on every platform, forever.
#
# Git's default on Windows (`core.autocrlf=true`) rewrites text files to CRLF on
# checkout. For this repo that is not cosmetic: `crates/fm-app/tests/fixtures/vault`
# holds hand-authored notes that the tests parse as bytes, and CI's first Windows run
# failed all eight `e2e_vault` tests because every fixture arrived with `\r\n`.
# `ci/checks.sh` and `packaging/*.sh` would break the same way in reverse.
#
# `from_file` tolerates CRLF regardless — a Windows user's editor produces it and their
# notes must still load — but a vault whose bytes change per platform is a vault whose
# byte-for-byte round-trip is a lie, so we don't let it happen here in the first place.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never touch these, whatever git guesses. A binary left off this list falls under `* text=auto
# eol=lf` above, which line-ending-normalizes its bytes on every checkout — so it can never
# byte-match its stored blob and shows as perpetually "modified" (this bit `icon.icns` until it was
# added here). Any new tracked binary asset must be listed.
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary

# Windows launchers are the one exception to LF-forever, and it is the platform's rule, not ours.
# `cmd.exe` parses a batch file line by line as it executes it, and an LF-only file can lose the
# last line or mis-parse; Windows Script Host is likewise happiest with CRLF. These two files are
# never read on Linux or macOS — they ship only in the windows-x86_64 archive — so nothing else
# sees the difference. Everything above still holds: notes and shell scripts stay LF everywhere.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.vbs text eol=crlf
